Abstract:
Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is the most frequent malignant tumor affecting the adult kidney. The incidence of RCC is increasing in many western countries. To date, there is no chemotherapy for the treatment of advanced kidney cancer with objective response rates higher than 15%. Recently, antibodies and small molecules targeting VEGFR and mTOR (Avastin, Temsirolimus, Sunitinib, Sorafenib) showed promising effects in patients with metastatic RCC. However, these treatments have significant toxic side effects and response is only seen in a subgroup of renal cancer patients. To further succeed in the treatment of RCC and to improve the prognosis of RCC patients, development of a serum test for the detection of RCC metastases as well as the identification of predictive markers for treatment reponse are of utmost importance. 
The proposed project is a continuation of previous SNF grants, which allowed the identification of novel molecular mechanisms involved in renal cancer initiation and metastasis.  Our novel project aims at at the translation of this knowledge into clinical practice. Loss of function of the von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) tumor suppressor due to mutation is characteristic for most cases of sporadic clear cell RCC (ccRCC). Work on the mechanism of action of the VHL product, pVHL, revealed that it is a multipurpose adapter protein involved in a variety of cellular processes including ubiquitin-mediated degradation of hypoxia-inducible factor ? (HIF?), stabilization and maintainance of primary cilia and promotion of proper spindle orientation and chromosomal stability. Loss of pVHL function leads to the deregulation of transcription regulatory pathways as well as of the cytoskeleton and the extracellular matrix, which play critical roles in cell cycle arrest, apoptosis, adhesion, metabolism, angiogenesis and metastasis. It has been shown that different VHL mutations trigger different signalling pathways. Moreover, in about 20% of ccRCC VHL is not affected suggesting the existence of other tumor suppressors whose loss of function leads to the same phenotypic output seen in VHL-dependent ccRCC. VHL-independent pathways have not yet been described. Detailed molecular understanding of the role of the pVHL-dependent and independent signaling circuitry in cancer will offer improved insights into the events leading to sporadic ccRCC. 
Hypothesis 
This proposal is based on the hypotheses 
1) that it is possible to develop a VHL-dependent serum signature for clear cell renal cancer, which will facilitate early detection of RCC and RCC metastasis in the blood;
2) that the VHL mutation type is relevant for the biological behaviour of clear cell RCC and potentially it’s response to targeted therapy;
3) that the characterization of novel, VHL-independent mechanisms allows further subgrouping of clear cell RCC. The knowledge of these subgroups will help to better understand the biological processes implicated in RCC which in turn will lead to improved individualized treatment strategies. 
Specific Aims
The promising results of previous pilot projects led to the description of 3 specific research subprojects, which aims at:
Part A)
- the establishment of a RCC serum barcode based on the identified RCC cell surfaceome;
- the functional analysis of cell surface glycoproteins for potential anti-tumor treatment.
Part B)
- the functional analysis of VHL mutations in RCC by focussing on their effects on pVHL/HIF? stability; 
- the correlation of the VHL mutation type with patient prognosis and response to targeted therapies.
Part C)
- the identification of VHL-independent molecular mechanisms in RCC, e.g. gene fusions, and transcriptional “chimerisms” in renal cancer using next-generation RNA sequencing;
- the validation of novel gene fusions and read through events by Q-PCR and FISH;
- the evaluation of the frequency of these events on tissue microarrays (TMA) with more than 1000 primary RCC and  more than 100 renal carcinoma metastases.

The primary interest of our lab is to investigate how individual naïve T cells give rise to different types of effector and memory T cell subsets. In particular, we are interested in characterizing the flexibility versus stability of various T cell lineages, including Th1 and T follicular helper (Tfh) cell fate. We have designed a novel vaccination strategy that induces the biased generation of TFH effector and memory cells.  By challenging vaccinated mice with chronic viral infection we demonstrated that the quality of the CD4 memory T cell compartment is an important mediator of host protection versus pathology. We further determined that TFH memory cells are long-lived, maintain extensive expansion/differentiation capacity, and express a unique metabolic gene signature typically associated with effector T cells and trained innate immune cells. Finally, we established a time-lapse microscopy acquisition and analysis pipeline to assess the dynamics of T cell differentiation in vitro. Using this platform, we have begun to tease apart the experimental conditions required for early T cell fate diversification by asymmetric division. Based on these observations we have 3 specific aims:

AIM 1: to examine the cellular mechanisms underlying vaccine induced TFH effector cell bias and subsequent host protection during chronic viral infection; 
AIM 2: to investigate the contribution of cAMP, cholesterol and amino acid transport in the maintenance and function of TFH memory cells; 
AIM 3: to determine the role of cell density/TCR signaling and inflammatory cytokines on asymmetric division and to assess the relationship between asymmetric division and T cell fate.

TrACE (Training Assessment Competencies in English as a Second Language) is a follow-up to two previous research projects in the area of English learning: ASSET (Assessing Student’s English Texts)  and MEWS (Measuring English Writing at Secondary Level) . While ASSET was concerned with teachers’ diagnostic competences when judging English student texts, MEWS generated a large corpus of authentic learner texts with holistic ratings by trained humans and assessment software. TrACE combines these projects to achieve following objectives: 
1) Generating detailed ratings of text qualities for a large corpus of authentic student texts, achieving highest possible objectivity by combining human ratings and machine scoring (Studies 1 & 2);
2)Investigating what factors at teacher, student, text, and judgment level determine the ability of pre-service and in-service English teachers to assess complex English learner texts accurately by comparing their assessment to human- and machine-made benchmark scores (Study 3);
3) Building a comprehensive, free online training tool and evaluating its effectiveness with a large sample of pre-service English teachers (Study 4, 5 & 6).
Empirical studies have shown scoring consistency to be relatively low when raters do not use analytic assessment practices (i.e., apply detailed criteria to student texts) (Dempsey et al., 2009). Therefore, in Study 1, human raters will assess a large portion of texts from the MEWS corpus using analytic criteria developed in ASSET, relating to aspects of textual quality typically assessed by teachers in schools (Keller, 2013). These ratings serve as the basis for Study 2, in which automatic scoring models for the analytic human scores are developed using natural language processing (NLP) techniques (Rupp et al., 2019). Combinations of human and machine ratings will then be used as benchmarks in several empirical studies that aim to train teachers’ diagnostic competences.
Working with a large corpus of authentic texts will allow us to investigate key determinants of judgment accuracy as continuous variables within one single study. Study 3 will investigate the influence of teacher, student, text, and judgments characteristics on teacher judgement accuracy (Südkamp et al., 2012). The same corpus is then used to investigate the effectiveness of specific training measures: Study 4 will examine whether teachers who receive feedback on their text judgments (comparison with benchmarks) become more accurate in their assessment. Studies 5 & 6 will provide teachers with automatically generated visualized feedback on key features of text quality to determine whether this helps them to assess texts more accurately (vocabulary in Study 5 and argumentative structure in Study 6). Based on the results of these training measures, we will develop a comprehensive, free on-line training tool for pre-service and in-service teachers to develop their diagnostic competence while using detailed analytic criteria. TrACE will thus explore the concordant variables of diagnostic competence in the domain of text assessment and make a substantial contribution to the field of teacher professionalization in a national and international context.

The attributes of seismic waves, such as velocity and polarization, or the wave-field composition reflect the properties of the propagation media. Analysis of the seismic wave-field therefore enables us to gather knowledge of geological and geophysical features of the Earth’s structure. Depending on the wave type (surface waves or body waves) and frequency range, different geophysical methods can be used to resolve different levels of details in the structure. Passive seismic surveying methods represent a cost-effective alternative to expensive invasive techniques (e.g. borehole techniques) of near surface exploration. Such methods therefore represent a valuable tool in geophysical and geotechnical investigations, especially related to earthquake risk reduction. During the past decade, passive methods based on the analysis of ambient vibration wave-fields have become popular due to their success and wide range applicability in a variety of situations. Single station methods are widely used to derive the fundamental frequency of resonance f0, whereas seismic arrays are used to derive shear-wave velocity profiles of the soil columns respectively. The basis of passive surface-wave array analysis is that the frequency dependence of the surface-wave phase velocity, or the ‘dispersion relation’, can be estimated using different signal processing techniques. A model of seismic wave velocities, compatible with the observed dispersion relation and f0, is then found by solving a non-linear geophysical inverse problem. Although passive methods have been continuously developed over the last decade, there is still considerable potential for improvement due to the complexity of the problem; as outlined in our proposal.
Hazards estimates are derived from ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) that often rely just on geological information to classify a station in terms of Vs30 or soil classes. This fact is one of the main reasons for large epistemic uncertainties in such relations and derived hazard products. Thus, there is an urgent need for cost effective procedures to derive local subsurface structure of the existing thousands of seismic stations worldwide. The methods developed within the framework of this project should help resolve these issues, improving the stations’ site classification, and therefore reducing uncertainties in hazard estimates. In particular, in this project we are interested in the development of signal processing methods for the analysis of ambient vibrations and earthquake ground motion from single stations and array recordings. Our aim is to significantly extend the analysis capabilities of the methods presently in use. The project’s primary focus lies in the methodological development of processing techniques and the application to specific products for seismic hazard assessment. Probabilistic graphical models, proposed in our project, are becoming more and more popular tools in various fields including machine learning and signal processing. In our approach we rely on factor graphs, one flavor among many graphical modeling techniques. The new wave-field decomposition method, developed recently, has several advantages when compared to other methods, and the potential for further developments is considerable. We envision improvements of this analysis method in different ways: we aim at the retrieval of additional geophysical properties of interest; to improve the accuracy of the methods; and to extend the domain of application of the passive methods to new types of measurements and depth ranges. We use both ambient vibrations and earthquake recordings. The specific goals of the project include:
1. Exploring tools to extract properties of the local structure from single station recordings;
2. Improving the accuracy of estimated wave-field parameters with arrays. This will be achieved by considering the broadband nature of seismic waves and by exploiting the time-invariability of seismic parameters over long recordings;
3. The estimation of additional quantities of interest with array recordings, such as surface wave attenuation (Q factor), directional properties of surface waves, and the composition of the wave-field;
4. Extending the domain of applications to larger arrays including temporary large arrays and data from the Swiss Seismic Networks; 
5. Application of the new methods to derive structural information for seismic stations in regional networks;

Early diagnosis and therapy increasingly operate at the cellular, molecular or even at the genetic level. As diagnostic techniques transition from the systems to the molecular level, the role of multi-modal imaging becomes increasingly important. Whole body hybrid (PET/CT and PET/MR) imaging, making use of the standardized uptake value (SUV), is well established in clinical setting for diagnosis and staging, treatment response monitoring and radiation therapy treatment planning of a wide range of oncologic malignancies. However, the SUV metric derived from static PET data does not capture the dynamics of the PET probe biodistribution in the body. Alternatively, dynamic PET imaging, presently employed mostly in research setting, is capable of tracing tracer kinetics thus allowing for more accurate quantification of tracer uptake. This acquisition mode enables to enhance, in certain situations, normal and abnormal (tumor) tissue characterization, assessment of treatment response or radiation therapy planning. However, given the long acquisition times, complexity of data analysis and the need to handle large data sets, dynamic data acquisition protocols have been confined to limited axial field-of-view (single-bed) coverage. As such, parametric imaging based on full kinetic analysis using spatiotemporal image reconstruction is rarely used due to noise induced bias and variance in kinetic parameters and analysis is usually restricted to semi-quantitative (SUV) indices.
We request funding to develop and validate in clinical setting a methodology to translate dynamic PET acquisition for 4D parametric imaging with motion compensation to routine clinical practice of whole-body PET-CT and PET-MR imaging for the assessment and characterization of disease spread and recurrence. This work will push forward the development and widespread adoption of dynamic whole body PET-CT and PET-MRI, the latter being an embryonic technology that has excellent potential to become a powerful tool for clinical research and diagnosis. This research involves medical physicists, physicians, radiologists, radiochemists and scientists working at the Department of Radiology and Medical Informatics of Geneva University Hospital (HUG), and image processing and analysis researchers from the University of Geneva (UniGe) and the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology (EPFL) in collaboration with international academic partners to achieve our goals.
The division of nuclear medicine together with national and international partners foresees to develop innovative approaches for motion compensated direct 4D parametric whole-body PET imaging. We submit this funding request to boost our research in this area with the goals to:

A.Perform Monte Carlo simulation studies to optimize the study design and the data acquisition and processing protocol by studying the effects of various parameters on imaging performance, and generation of dynamic PET data sets corresponding to the geometry of our PET-CT and PET-MRI systems;
B.Develop new, more accurate and robust 4D image reconstruction algorithm along with a 2-tissue compartment model to directly estimate parametric images of microparameters on dynamic whole-body [18F]-FDG PET data and compare the generated parametric maps with those obtained from traditional post-reconstructed kinetic analysis;
C.Implement and evaluate a new approach to direct parameter estimation by incorporating a recently proposed kinetic modeling strategy within a direct 4D image reconstruction to reduce error propagation from erroneous model fits, thus improving kinetic parameter precision and accuracy;
D.Develop and validate novel 4D MR-derived motion correction schemes to eliminate artefacts seen in PET-CT by developing MR-based motion-compensated PET attenuation correction strategies;
E.Develop new and robust multivariate and multiparametric PET image analysis tools and validate the developed quantitative PET imaging methodology using simulated and experimental dynamic phantom studies and clinical data and apply it in a clinical environment in various oncologic applications.

Knowledge of the timing of fluid flow and exhumation in a collisional orogen like the Alps is crucial for the understanding of different low temperature processes (e.g., element transport, exhumation versus cooling rates). In the proposed study we will use high-resolution (~10µm; ~0.1 Ma) ion microprobe (SwissSIMS) Th-U-Pb isotopic age dating of monazite from Alpine clefts and combine it with oxygen isotope analyses and different types of monazite thermometry, in order to constrain the temperature and the time point when meteoric fluid reached the cleft level. Data obtained in the first part of the project shows that monazite crystallization in Alpine clefts occurred mainly due to tectonically driven dissolution/precipitation events between ~90 Ma to ~6 Ma, with crystallization durations lasting up to ~20 Ma. Our data also indicate that cleft monazite grows at temperatures around or below the zircon fission track closure temperature and above the (U-Th)/He zircon closure temperature, thus at much lower temperatures than typical retrograde mineral reactions in rock. The proposed study will mainly be conducted on samples already available using a combination of field work, optical techniques, Raman microprobe, secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS), electron microprobe, electron microscope, laser ablation inductively coupled mass spectrometry (LA-ICPMS) and laser fluorination oxygen isotope analysis. 
We ask for the extension of 1 year for the present PhD student Christian Bergemann and for a new PhD student that will assure a smooth transition of experience in the monazite preparation and dating techniques.  The main tasks for the new PhD student will be: 
(1) Obtaining SIMS monazite growth domain ages for the Argentera massif, Alpine high-pressure regions, the Western Tauern Window and the Aar- and Gotthard Massifs, to link them with tectonic events, and to compare them with zircon fission track and (U-Th)/He data; 
(2) Using oxygen isotopes in monazite growth domains to constrain the origin of the fluid (rock-buffered versus meteoric);
(3) Constraining monazite growth temperatures by using monazite-quartz and other oxygen isotope thermometers, Ti-in-quartz thermometry in TiO2-buffered monazite associations, and fluid inclusions;
(4) Studying in selected cases the interaction of host-rock REE minerals and cleft monazite, in order to better understand fluid-rock interaction.

For plants, nitrogen (N) is quantitatively the most important nutrient acquired from soil, assimilated in roots or shoots, reallocated between organs, and used for growth, development and yield. Inorganic forms of N, i.e. nitrate and ammonium, are considered to be the main N sources taken up by plants, but also organic N forms occur in soil, often in greater amounts than inorganic N. There is clear evidence that plant can use soluble organic N compounds including amino acids and small peptides, which are common in natural ecosystems and in agricultural systems in certain situations. However, plants differ in their ability to acquire and metabolise organic N. To foster growth and yield, modern agriculture applies N mainly as mineral fertiliser. But with less than 50% of fertiliser-N recovered by crops, the remainder can be lost from soil as leachate and run-off (especially mobile nitrate) or gaseous N (including the potent greenhouse gas nitrous oxide) which pollutes the environment and threatens human health. The interest in organic N as a source for plants and as a component of next-generation fertilisers is driven by the urgent need for sustainable agricultural practices and a reduced N footprint. Halving nitrogen waste and achieving a circular N economy - priority goals of the United Nations Environment Assembly - require a shift towards organic N supplementing mineral N fertilisers. This means that future crops need competency for using both inorganic and organic N, which demands understanding of the genes and their variations to support future crop selection and breeding.

The aim of the proposed research is therefore to expand current knowledge on the ability of plants to use organic N for growth by exploiting natural variation of organic N efficiency in Arabidopsis. This research will use genome wide association studies (GWAS) and examine loci associated with the most significant SNPs (single nucleotide polymorphisms) to identify new genes and alleles responsible for altered organic N use efficiency. The research focuses on the plant’s ability to use amino acids, a common form of organic N in soil, and on transporting large oligopeptides into cell vacuoles, which is likely crucial for internal cycling of N. The proposed research is based on our discovery that Arabidopsis genotypes differ in their ability to use amino acids for growth and that multiple loci are involved, which manifests in certain genotypes producing up to 30-times more biomass than others when grown with amino acids as sole N source. The objectives are to:

Objective 1
(i)Explore the natural genetic variation of Arabidopsis accessions to use amino acids as N source;
(ii)Characterise the genetic architecture of organic N use, i.e. the heritability, number of loci, their effect and allele frequencies;
(iii)Identify candidate genes associated with the most significant SNPs; 
(iv)Characterise the function and role of these novel genes or alleles for organic N utilisation;
Objective 2
(v)Investigate the role of potential vacuolar oligopeptide transporters for intracellular N- cycling.

The experimental design and analysis pipeline for GWAS for screening large numbers of plants has already been established, the plant material is available and image analysis has been automated, allowing rapid progress. The knowledge gained will shed light on the importance of organic N for plant productivity and growth and contribute to better understand evolutionary adaptations of plants in ecological contexts, i.e. adaptation to environments with different soil N forms. We further anticipate that the research will generate knowledge for applications in crops, including future plant selection and breeding that aims to improve N use efficiency in agriculture, with anticipated greater N recycling and formulations of sustainable N fertilisers.

The general theme of this project is to develop new directions for quadratic optimisation problems in mathematical finance for frictionless models of financial markets. We plan to combine the analytical tractability of quadratic criteria with new challenges arising from recent improved modelling approaches to make new progress and obtain new insights. More specifically, we intend to study
A) mean-variance hedging in models with rough volatility;
B) time-consistency and dynamic programming for mean-variance portfolio selection;
C) mean-variance hedging and mean-variance portfolio selection in the context of robust finance.

While aerosol particles cool down the atmosphere by scattering light and acting as cloud condensation nuclei (CCN), their impact remains the single highest uncertainty in quantifying the climate sensitivity to anthropogenic greenhouse gases. A great challenge in understanding their impact resides in identifying the processes by which new particles form and grow from oxidized vapours into cloud condensation nuclei. While sulphuric acid is recognized as a main ingredient for new particle formation, the observed nucleation rates at many sites cannot be explained solely by this molecule. The development of chemical ionization mass spectrometers have advanced the detection of nucleating clusters, providing compelling evidence that amines and highly oxygenated organic vapours may participate in new particle formation. However, the role of these different ingredients in forming new particles is still poorly known, especially in the free troposphere where 35% of CCN is formed. 
My goal for this project is to further understand and quantify at a molecular level the various processes and mechanisms that govern new-particle formation in the free troposphere. Therefore I propose a field study with state-of-the-art instruments at a selected high-altitude location, complementary to the Jungfraujoch and the Pyramid stations. From the comprehensive data sets I expect to constrain the chemistry driving free-tropospheric new-particle formation, and to answer the following questions: Are sulfuric acid concentrations high enough to enable nucleation on its own? Do ammonia or amines contribute to nucleation at this altitude far away from their sources? Can we observe pure organic nucleation? Are organosulfates or organonitrates essential contributors for new particle formation? Are any organic compounds involved of biogenic or anthropogenic origin? What drives particle growth to 3 nm? To answer these questions this proposal has three key scientific objectives:

•To determine the chemical nature of naturally charged and neutral clusters at a high-altitude site complementary to the two sites I have studied previously;
•To establish a quantitative link between detected clusters and newly formed particles;
•To identify the impact of environmental conditions on the cluster composition, by comparing results from four different sites around the world.

The research will be implemented at the University of Helsinki, where the excellent infrastructure will insure the successful achievement of the project goals. The project will also greatly benefit from my knowledge in organizing campaigns in remote areas at high altitude. Helsinki will provide the state-of the art instrumentation mentioned above to measure the chemical composition of the nucleating clusters. The step after is to move to the Carnegie Mellon University and perform laboratory experiment that will help in the understanding of the ambient observations.

The main aim of the proposed project is to test the possibility of making the established philosophy journal "dialectica" open access, by developing new and innovative ways of digitally presenting scientific research and to develop new models for the open, participatory and productive sharing of the outcomes of scientific work and to generate input, data and proposals for best practice standards for the editing of academically ambitious and internationally recognised Open Access journals of the highest quality. 
Concretely, the study consists in expanding and transforming http://dialectica.philosophie.ch in three ways and with respect to three groups of stakeholders in scientific publishing: 
1. to readers, to offer a platform to discuss journal articles, responses and replies to them, to make available referee reports and critical discussions, to link journal articles with their genealogy and to offer bibliographic information of high quality; 
2. to authors, to make available a repository of information relating to the publication process in general and to the inner workings of the editorial committee of dialectica in particular, to incite an open, critical and informed discussion of publication practices, to offer the opportunity to respond to referee reports and to provide background information; 
3. to editors, to establish a network of exchange about editorial practices, Open Access policies, relations to commercial publishers, authorial and editorial misconduct, with a view to establish a common code of best practices. 
Concretely, the aims are (i) to develop new ways, based on LaTeX and its hyperref-package, to add additional value to pdf documents, in particular with respect to bibliographic information; (ii) to find more attractive ways of presenting scientific content on webpages, in particular if these are read on smartphones; (iii) to create a database of OA-funding possibilities and to demonstrate to other journals, in particular in philosophy and other disciplines of the humanities, the feasibility of going open access.

Weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) dark matter has a small but non-zero cross-section with standard model physics. Gamma-rays from dark matter annihilations could then be observable by gamma-ray telescopes such as Fermi and used to constrain the annihilation cross-section.
Indeed, observations of dwarf spheroidal galaxies with Fermi-LAT have ruled out the thermal relict cross-section for WIMPs of masses up to 10 GeV for the bb channel.
Studies of the extra galactic gamma-ray background (EGB) have yielded weaker limits so far. By cross correlating the EGB with tracers of dark matter, these limits can however be vastly improved.

In this project I will cross correlate the EGB with cosmic shear data from CFHTLenS and upcoming RCSLenS, building on previous work by Shirasaki et al. 
Previous limits obtained from cross correlating cosmic shear with gamma-rays will be improved by 

- carefully modelling the astrophysical contributions to the EGB;

- comparing different models of dark matter clustering and assessing their impact on the prediction of the gamma-ray flux from dark matter annihilations;

- investigating the impact of baryons on the clustering of dark matter;

- using the redshift information in the lensing data for a tomographic approach to disentangle the signal of dark matter annihilations from astrophysical sources;

- restricting masking of resolved gamma-ray point sources to only those whose redshift overlap with the lensing data;

- employing advanced cuts to the gamma-ray data to improve the directional accuracy of the photons;

- applying the analysis to RCSLenS data.

Using these techniques and extended data-sets it will be possible to rule out low mass WIMPs up to masses of 100 GeV. These limits derive from the analysis of the EGB and are thus complementary to searches for dark matter signatures from local sources such as the galactic centre, galactic halo, and dwarf spheroidal galaxies.

The present project aims at gathering scientific information on the environmental fate and effects of cerium oxide nanoparticles (CeO2NP). Cerium oxide is industrially exploited because of its catalytic properties also as diesel fuel additive. Due to the wide application of CeO2 nanoparticles and likelihood of release in the environment, investigations of their fate and effects is required to assist in evaluating their potential risks. 

Specific objectives of the project are:
•to examine how physicochemical characteristics of CeO2NP are influenced by interaction with phototrophic organisms;
•to examine the bioavailability and toxicity of CeO2NP to phototrophic organisms;
•to assess which physicochemical characteristics of the NP are determinant for their bioavailability and toxicity.
The project adopts a multidisciplinary approach combining chemical, biological and particle-analysis expertise. The ultimate purpose of the project is to contribute to laying a solid foundation that supports environmental risk assessment.

The propagation of waves in homogeneous and inhomogeneous physical space is a fundamental natural phenomenon, whose physical understanding, mathematical modeling, and reliable numerical simulation are of immense impact for important advances in areas as divers as medical technology, fast data transmission, noise control, and radar/sonar detection to name some but few of them.
    In this research project our goal is to develop stable formulations and advanced numerical methods for the modelling of high-frequency scattering by boundary integral equation methods. This is a topic of vivid research in applied and numerical mathematics with the wave propagation in homogeneous media subject to uniform radiating conditions as the most common paradigm. However for most practical applications, the ambient physical medium is non-homogeneous and consists of several regions with different acoustic properties and non-uniform radiation conditions. Typical examples include earth/air/water/layered soil which are characterized by different propagation parameters. Hence, the development and analysis of fast numerical discretization methods for wave propagation in absorbing half space and layered materials is a key step from the homogeneous, uniform case towards these important applications. The theoretical part in this project has the aim to derive well-posed boundary integral equations which are equivalent to the original Helmholtz equations with variable material coefficients in the d-dimensional physical space, d?{2,3,…}. For the development and analysis of the numerical discretization, we investigate two model problems which are subject to piecewise constant material coefficients in a layered or half-space geometrical setting; more complicated geometries and variable coefficients are out of the scope of this project; however we are convinced that the envisaged achievements can pave the way for further developments towards this goal at large.
    In our project the main research goal is to develop and analyze fast numerical methods for the reliable simulation of high frequency scattering problems. For homogeneous media and uniform radiation conditions the arising integral operator is characterized by an explicitly known kernel function and almost all fast numerical techniques for its discretization are based on this explicit form. For half-space problems with absorbing boundary conditions or layered media problems such an explicit representation is not feasible. In this project, our goal is to extend the theory and development of fast numerical methods for scattering problems to these two important new applications. This requires

1.the derivation of a formulation of the governing partial differential equations (PDEs) (for the Helmholtz equation with variable coefficients in d spatial dimensions as a standard paradigm) as boundary integral equations so that its stability is inherited from the well-posedness of the PDE;
2.the derivation of new representations of the kernel functions which allows for a fast evaluation and efficient approximation;
3.the development and analysis of numerical methods for the approximation of the elements in the system matrix and the construction of sparse DH² approximation of boundary integral operators involving these Green's functions.

This project combines Raman lidar measurements of water vapor and particle backscatter with frost point hygrometer and backscatter sonde measurements to derive midlatitude ice cloud properties including the in-cloud humidity.  Processes in ice clouds will be constrained by tracking the evolution of cloud backscatter and humidity in an unprecedented manner by obtaining “cloud matches”, i.e. measuring the same cloud-filled air parcel twice.  Match points are some 10 to 40 minutes apart and with a match error of less than 2 km.  Cloud matches will be analyzed systematically by sophisticated size-resolving cloud modeling, using the full air mass column with a vertical resolution of 20-50 m.  The projects intends to deliver 36 such cloud matches (one per month), which will allow to obtain a statistically sound analysis 

The general goal is a better understanding of microphysical and radiative processes of tropospheric aerosol and cloud particles, in particular concerning the conditions leading to supersaturation with respect to ice and impeded dehydration of the upper troposphere.  The work proposed will contribute to the closure of the following questions:  Are super- and subsaturations as observed within midlatitude ice clouds compatible with common (textbook) knowledge?  Specifically, do they equilibrate within expected times or do we need non-conservative explanations (such as minute mass accommodation)?  Can we better constrain the magnitude and effect of small-scale temperature fluctuations?  Furthermore, how important is the presence of ice nuclei in midlatitude cirrus clouds?
The proposed research project will approach these questions by addressing the following objectives: 
-analyze Lidar measurements over Payerne, Switzerland (with the Raman Lidar for Meteorological Observations, RALMO) to characterize cirrus cloud occurrence, altitude and morphology; 
-perform monthly radio soundings from Payerne using SnowWhite chilled mirror frost point hygrometers in tandem with COBALD (Compact Optical AerosoL Detector) backscatter sondes on the same payload, occasionally together with a modified Kipp and Zonen CNR4 net radiometer;
-analyze all data statistically to assess the frequency distribution of relative humidity with respect to ice over Switzerland in cloud free and cloudy environments;
-identify cloud matches between RALMO (upstream) and the SnowWhite/COBALD tandem (down-stream) from high spatial and temporal resolution air parcel trajectories based on the COSMO model to investigate cloud evolution;
-analyze the cloud match data using the Zurich Optical and Microphysical Model (ZOMM) to determine whether the observed supersaturated cases can be reconciled with expected microphysical behavior, whether heterogeneous nucleation plays a role for the cirrus formation, and if so at which number concentration of heterogonous ice nuclei;
-scrutinize data quality, especially with respect to potential measurement errors and uncertainties of SnowWhite and RALMO;
-examine the tropospheric backscatter and water vapor profiles to derive the radiative effect of aerosol and clouds encountered in the soundings, and compare the radiative impact modelled with ZOMM and inferred from the measured radiometer profiles.

The specific aims of this work will develop and understand directed colloidal self-assembly using pulsed and modulated electric fields. The proposed work will focus on two specific goals: 
(1) We will study the microstructure of suspensions that form under toggled and modulated al-ternating current (ac) electric fields; 
(2) We will study the effect of particle shape as a model of more complex interactions between particles on the self-assembly process;

Understanding of the future change in climate is a problem of utmost importance for the scientific community, society and policymakers. The influence of powerful volcanic eruptions on climate was recognized long ago. Recently, it was suggested that small volcanic eruptions can also influence climate. However, projections of future climate change in the recent and upcoming IPCC reports do not take into account any effects from either infrequent, explosive or more-numerous small volcanic eruptions, rendering our knowledge of future climate far from complete. 

We propose to address this missing component in future climate change projections. We will design a set of future scenarios of volcanic activity, simulate the effect of both large and small volcanic eruptions, integrate these scenarios into the standard set of future climate projections and investigate their potential effects on extreme weather events and food production. The main objectives of the project are to:
•Design future scenarios of volcanic activity that include eruptions of all intensities;
•Evaluate the influence of volcanic eruptions on long-term changes in major climate indicators, climate extremes and food production;
•Compare the effect of volcanic eruptions with other natural and anthropogenic factors influencing climate;
•Characterize and understand both the mechanisms responsible for the downward propagation of stratospheric disturbances resulting from volcanic eruptions, as well as their influence on the predictability of weather and climate. 

To achieve these goals, we will make use of our coupled atmosphere-ocean-aerosol-chemistry-climate model (AOACCM). This model provides a powerful tool to investigate interaction between sulfate aerosols, atmospheric chemistry, ocean dynamics and climate. The sulfate aerosol part of the model, developed in our group, is capable of explicitly calculating the sulfate aerosol properties and therefore treating all kind of volcanic eruptions as well as other sources of sulfur containing emissions. Our project will lead to the first comprehensive assessment of the long-term natural influence of volcanic eruptions in future climate projections, their uncertainties and their impact on extreme weather events. The work will impact the future IPCC and WMO/UNEP ozone assessments and will provide better information for society and policymakers.

The overarching goals of the proposed project are to develop a new in situ tribological method, based on high pressure X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (HPXPS), and to use it to establish the scientific foundation of the influence of the environment on the tribological performance of diamond (highly sp3-bonded, nearly hydrogen-free carbon films, either amorphous or ultrananocrystalline) and diamond-like carbon (hydrogenated sp2-bonded carbon) solid lubricant coatings as well as their derivatives (e.g., diamond-like nanocomposites, DLNs). These materials are amongst the strongest and stiffest thin film materials known, and can exhibit extremely low friction and wear without any added lubricant, making them extremely promising for a wide range of applications. However, this behaviour is limited by environmental effects, whose physical origins are not understood.

To accomplish these goals, I will pursue the following specific objectives:
1)incorporation of a vacuum-compatible computer-controlled pin-on-disk tribometer into an HPXPS system. This system is unique in the world as it is the only laboratory-based XPS system that can work at elevated gas pressures (up to at least 0.1 Torr);
2)apply this newly developed in situ tribological test system to two well-known model coatings (i.e. diamond-like carbon (DLC) and ultrananocrystalline diamond (UNCD)) in order to prove the usefulness of the analytical approach. This will also provide the opportunity to gain new in situ spectroscopic insights into the surface-chemical mechanisms underlying the well-known differences in the tribological performance of DLC and UNCD in different environments;
3)perform in situ tribological tests for throwing experimental light on the fundamental mechanism leading to the excellent tribological performance of diamond-like carbon nanocomposites (DLNs) in different environments. This will be done by carrying out experiments varying the critical environmental and tribological conditions, such as applied load, sliding speed, temperature and partial pressure of water and molecular oxygen or hydrogen;
4)determination of the local morphology and of the changes in the nano-scale properties (e.g., adhesion and friction) of worn vs. unworn regions by atomic force microscopy (AFM).

The potential of the new in situ tribological approach is considered very significant, both in view of the exciting insights into the fundamental mechanism underlying the outstanding tribological performance of hard carbon-based films, and in view of the unique possibility of applying, for the first time, one of the most important surface analytical developments emerging in the last decade, i.e. HPXPS, to in situ tribology.

Precipitation and dissolution reactions in porous media dominate and control a large number of geochemical processes and industrial applications that span from geothermal energy and oil industry to pharmaceutical products, batteries, catalysts and to long-term nuclear waste containment. The precipitation-dissolution of minerals from aqueous solutions alters the pore space and its connectivity in a way that has a complex feedback to the ion transport in aqueous phase itself. Reactive transport models and simulations are providing the framework to understand and predict such phenomena. Very often, simulations are done at the macroscopic level (field scale) using a simplified description of the processes that actually take place at the microscale. For example, the change in permeability and diffusivity of a porous medium due to reactions is correlated to a global change of the bulk porosity in the medium, using empirical laws with adjustable parameters. There are clear evidences that the macroscopic properties of the medium depend on the physical and chemical processes that occur at the micropore scale in a strongly non-linear way. It is therefore not surprising, that smooth simplified relations, frequently are inadequate to predict accurately the temporal evolution of the system of interest. It is the microscopic physics per se that ultimately control the macroscopic processes. Fundamental in-depth understanding and accurate prediction of the underlying processes can be enhanced by direct pore-scale modeling, supported by experimental investigations.
This project aims at:
(i)combining a state-of-the-art reactive transport algorithm with classical nucleation theory concept;
(ii)modelling competing precipitation mechanisms along with dissolution in realistic systems;
(iii)using advanced synchrotron-based X-ray techniques to gain insight of the micro-meter level processes;
(iv)validating the resulting numerical modelling framework against reactive transport experiments.
The modelling activities will be based on the lattice Boltzmann (LB) numerical method and will be supported with results obtained from reactive transport experiments and advanced instrumental diagnostics with special focus to the celestite-barite system. The selection of the celestite-barite system is primarily motivated by the environmental relevance of barite scale formation in industrial applications related to large scale geochemical systems (oil exploration, geothermal heat extraction, uranium mining) and by the availability of an exceptionally complete set of thermodynamic and kinetic data. Moreover, results and samples of relevant reactive transport experiments have been already produced in-house, in the framework of a recently completed PhD thesis (2016). Therein, a simple system consisting of reactive celestite (SrSO4) intermixed with a comparatively inert matrix (quartz sand) was selected. By saturating the pore space with barium chloride solution (BaCl2), celestite is partially dissolved and replaced by the more insoluble barite (BaSO4) thus modifying the hydraulic properties of the medium (porosity, permeability, connectivity). 
Further characterization of experimental results will be carried out and analyzed at the Swiss Light Source (SLS) synchrotron, by means of advanced synchrotron-based X-ray techniques, including micro X-ray diffraction/fluorescence and 3D-microtomography. The prospected methodology will be able to describe, as a function of time and at high spatial resolution (micrometer scale), a number of observable processes and mechanisms, such as precipitation via homogeneous (nano-crystalline) and heterogeneous nucleation (epitaxial growth). 
The obtained results are expected to provide fundamental insights on the microscopic mechanisms governing dissolution-precipitation reactions and on how these mechanisms are affecting bulk hydraulic properties in geochemically relevant porous media.

The ATLAS experiment is one of the two large general purpose experiments at CERN's Large Hadron Collider (LHC). ATLAS has operated with outstanding performance and produced numerous physics results, which include the discovery of the Higgs boson in 2012. Following that discovery, the physics program of the experiment focuses on measurements of the properties of the new particle, measurements of the Standard Model particles and interactions and searches for new phenomena which could give answers to the open questions that the Standard Model leaves unanswered. The searches have so far revealed no compelling indication for new physics, resulting in stringent constraints set to exotic models and theories.

The measurements of the properties of the Standard Model constituents, including those of the Higgs boson, become more and more crucial as deviations from predictions can hint new physics. Large precision has been achieved in many production processes and decay channels of the Higgs boson, leading to precise measurements of couplings consistent with the Standard Model. Some of the most important properties, however, require further precision. Such properties are the top and bottom Yukawa couplings, the dominant ones among the Higgs couplings, as well as the Higgs self-coupling. The former two can be simultaneously measured via ttH production with H to bb. The latter, HH production, has largest branching ratios for H to bb, leading to a four b-quark signature. These couplings are the least constrained ones due to experimental difficulties associated with the final states, but also due to the low cross-section with which the production is occurring at the LHC. The High-Luminosity LHC upgrade (HL-LHC), envisaged for 2025, is planned to deliver an order of magnitude more data than previous runs, giving access to processes that were previously inaccessible, such as the HH production.

Both in the current LHC phase and in the future HL-LHC, enhanced phase-space in triggering, better reconstruction, better understanding of the background processes and improved methods to measure them are key aspects towards achieving the desired measurements of the Higgs couplings. The present funding request targets exactly these aspects. In the twenty-month period, from 02/2018 till 10/2019, we will:
- improve flavor tagging using particle flow, with a direct impact to the H to bb mass resolution;
- study the background processes tt plus (heavy flavor) jets and ttZ, in the fully hadronic final states, which we know well from the SUSY multi-jet searches we are currently pursuing;
- evaluate, at HL-LHC conditions, the sensitivity of ttH and HH in fully hadronic channels and produce combinations with other channels, particularly in the challenging case of HH;
- improve, at the HL-LHC ATLAS trigger and data acquisition system, the currently compromised hadronic trigger capability using track-based pile-up mitigation methods implemented at hardware; develop simulation and performance studies, as well as a demonstrator to test firmware, algorithms and resources, in the laboratory and in testbeam.

To achieve these goals, we are asking SNF to finance a postdoctoral researcher for twenty months as well as two Ph.D. students for the same period. The experience gained by this work will be the stepping stone and a solid basis for further research that will span from measurements of Higgs couplings with LHC data to the construction of a hardware track trigger for the HL-LHC ATLAS upgrade.

The present project aims at gathering scientific information on the environmental fate and effects of silver- and cerium oxide nanoparticles. Silver NPs belong to the most important materials incorporated in consumer products and used because of their toxicity to microorganisms. Cerium oxide is industrially exploited because of its catalytic properties also as diesel fuel additive. Due to the wide application of Ag and CeO2 nanoparticles and likelihood of release in the environment, investigations of their fate and effects is required to assist in evaluating their role.
Specific objectives of the project are:
•to examine how physicochemical characteristics of CeO2NPs are influenced by factors relevant for natural waters;
•to examine the toxicity of AgNPs and CeO2NP s to algae;
•to assess which physicochemical characteristics of the NPs are determinant for toxicity.
The project adopts a multidisciplinary approach combining chemical, biological and particle-analysis expertise. The ultimate purpose of the project is to contribute to laying a solid foundation that supports environmental risk assessment.

Although bedload transport is a longstanding problem that has attracted considerable attention over the last decades, our understanding and capacity of prediction remain partial. Engineers are not really armed to provide long-term solutions that optimize sediment-routing issues, protection against floods, and restoration of ecosystems in complex rivers, in particular in mountain areas. For instance, in Switzerland, three mitigation campaigns have been undertaken since the late 19th century to ``tame'' the Rhone River in the Canton du Valais. Until very recently, research has been driven by the belief that sediment motion is controlled by water stream and therefore must be closely related to water discharge or flow depth. In recent years, evidence has however accumulated that on different time and space scales, sediment transport is a highly intermittent, nonlinear process. Occasionally, during some events (e.g., intense floods), bedload transport may become more continuous, which gives rise to a neater relation between water and solid discharges, where fluctuations are less marked.

The objective of this research program is to develop a hybrid deterministic-stochastic model of bedload transport in mountain streams (i.e., streams, whose bed gradient exceeds a few percent and bed is made up of coarse particles). The shallow-water equations constitute the deterministic component, while bedload transport is modelled using a stochastic population exchange model that we have recently developed. This model performs well for bed-equilibrium and steady-flow conditions. Theory will be extended to cope with more realistic flow conditions, in particular by considering the coupling between water flows, bed evolution, and sediment entrainment/deposition. These theoretical developments raise a number of issues: how to solve the coupled set of equations describing how a water stream interacts with a mobile bed while accounting for the stochastic behaviour of bedload transport? Are the laboratory experiments (used for testing theory) representative of full-scale processes? Shall we distinguish between intermittent and continuous sediment-transport processes or are they governed by the same physics?

To answer these questions (and others), the project includes a comprehensive experimental campaign involving 2 PhD students (only the thesis related to the current proposal is described) and hinges on 5 main work packages (WP1 to WP3 are at the heart of the PhD thesis for which we ask for funding):
 [--WP1]  laboratory experiments carried out in a straight flume to obtain new data sets for bed evolution and particle entrainment/deposition for steep coarse-grained bed and nonuniform flow conditions, i.e. cyclic hydrographs;
 [--WP2]  the same experiments as for WP1, but with a flume presenting a slope break and thus allowing for different forms of aggradation and degradation along the flume;
 [--WP3]  the  field survey of a 5-km reach of a mountain stream (Navisence River, Zinal, Valais) with emphasis given to the relation between bed morphologies and particle transport (measured under a bridge), a task under the supervision of Dr. Eric Bardou from CREALP (Sion);
 [--WP4]      the extension of our hybrid deterministic-stochastic model to cope with nonuniform flow conditions  and  development of a simplified version better suited to steep channels (use of the kinematic wave equation rather than the full Saint-Venant equations);
 [--WP5]   numerical simulations with a comparison between the fully deterministic model (based on the Saint-Venant-Exner equations), our hybrid stochastic-deterministic model (the Exner equation being replaced by an equivalent stochastic differential equation) and its simplified variant (WP4). This last task is a joint work with Prof. Patricio Bohórquez from the University of Jaén (Spain).

The requested financial support for this project is for one doctoral student (who should mainly work on experiments) and three months for an invited professor (Patricio Bohórquez).

The mechanical properties of lanthanum transition metal oxide perovskites at high temperatures have not received much attention, despite the fact that the high-temperature applications of these types of material demand certain electrochemical, catalytic and mechanical properties, such as mechanical strength and high creep resistance. The goal of the proposed research is to study the mechanical behavior of LaCoO3 and La0.8Ca0.2CoO3 under four point bending using incremental cyclic loading at room and high temperatures. This will have significant importance for understanding the mechanical behavior of these materials at high temperatures.
The objectives of the proposed project are to:
- study mechanical properties of the perovskites at room and high temperatures in general (elastic modulus, ultimate stress, fracture strain);
- analyse fracture surface of the perovskites specimens tested;
- evaluate mechanical behavior of LaCoO3 and La0.8Ca0.2CoO3 under four point bending using incremental cyclic loading at room and high temperatures;
- determine hysteresis area, irreversible strain, maximum strain, and elastic modulus of LaCoO3 and La0.8Ca0.2CoO3 upon heating and cooling
- study creep behavior of LaCoO3 and La0.8Ca0.2CoO3 at room and at high temperatures.
The elastic modulus at room and at high temperature will be determined using both stress-strain diagram and impulse excitation technique. Fractographic analysis will be performed on selected specimens by using of Scanning Electron Microscopy.

This proposal describes the extension of the project Humanitics (Humanitics: Visual Anlytics for All) for which a budget of 2 years has been granted to cover a PhD thesis. An extension of 2 years should enable the PhD student to finish the work started.

The initial goal of this project was to develop visual analytics tools for public organizations to enhance knowledge discovery, analytical capabilities, decision making, information exchange and communication. The targeted users are from countries all around the world, including least developed countries that generally do not have access to this type of technology. Early in the project we decided to focus on the visualization of spatial interaction information, and especially, on migrations flows. There are many important datasets with such information, which are available to the public including those collected by the UN. However, tools and techniques, which allow visualizing and analyzing spatial interactions, and especially, their changes over time, are not widely available and the subject matter remains underdeveloped.

In the past one and a half year the PhD student working on this project has achieved the following tasks:
- Acquisition and preparation of the datasets with migration flows (UNHCR refugee dataset, US migrations, Swiss commuters, etc);
- Creation of a platform for the visualization and exploration of spatial interaction information (JFlowMap);
- Development of a flowmap visualization and various interaction techniques for the exploration of migration flows;
- Implementation of several algorithms for reducing the cluttering in flow map visualizations;

Although in the first part of the project we have done quite some development on space-centric visualization and mining techniques to analyze migration flows, we just started developing time-centric abstract views to observe changes over time. Further, space and time dimensions are currently totally disconnected and one of the most important challenge of this project is to combine spatial and temporal information in a single interactive tool. This extension will concentrate of the following research tasks:
- Develop effective visualization and mining techniques facilitating the exploration of the development in time which preserve the spatial properties of the sources and destinations of the flows;
- Perform case studies with different spatial interaction datasets; •Evaluate qualitatively and quantitatively evaluate the effectiveness of the implemented techniques for solving specific user tasks involving the end users (analysts).

The present proposal has for aim to improve our knowledge on the following topics : 

1) How do stars form? The specific questions we want to address are the following ones : a) what is the link between the lifetime of the accretion disk, the evolution of the surface rotation of the nascent star and the possibility for planets to form?  b) How angular momentum of a forming massive star evolves during the accretion phase and how this evolution varies as a function of the accretion rate and of the metallicity? 

2) How do rotation and magnetic fields affect stars? We shall explore the effects of magnetic braking on massive star evolution. The consequences of such a process on the evolution with time of the surface velocity, of the surface abundances and on the global angular momentum content  of the star will be studied;  

3) How do the lives of massive stars end? Many new observational constraints are available on the frequency of different types of core collapse supernovae in galaxies. Our stellar models provide theoretical frames for understanding the links between metallicity, initial rotation, activity of a dynamo mechanism and the nature of the presupernova and of the core collapse event from single stars; 

4) What controls the mass, radius and spin of compact stellar remnants? Using new and updated grids of stellar models we shall provide for different metallicities the properties of the cores of stars for progenitors of white dwarfs, neutron stars and black-holes. The results of these models will be compared with the observed rotation rates of young white dwarfs, neutron stars and with the conditions required to produce gamma ray bursts; 

5) Nucleosynthesis and massive star populations in galaxies : new stellar models accounting for the best physics at the moment will be computed in order to provide theoretical predictions for various observed number ratios of massive stars in galaxies; 

6) Nucleosynthesis of the first stars: we shall explore the effects of rotation and magnetic fields on the evolution and the nucleosynthesis of the first stellar generations in the Universe; 

7) Asteroseismology : we shall continue to use this technic to probe the interiors of stars using the data collected by ground based spectroscopy and by the CoRoT and Kepler satellites; 

8) Exploring new physics in stars : we shall study the effects of WIMPS in stars, focusing on those stars found in the galactic centre where the density of WIMPS is higher than in the solar vicinity.

While the relationship between the U.S. and China is the world’s most critical, the future of this ‘great power dyad’ is highly unclear. Nationalism, which now appears to be on the rise in both the U.S. and China, puts central cooperative principles in U.S.-Chinese economic relations at serious risk. Domestically, identity politics, emphasis on international political competition, and vilification of foreign trade partners are trademarks of nationalist policies that become increasingly popular. This makes it much harder to maintain public support for open economy politics, particularly in the U.S. The reason is that domestic effects of economic openness become less relevant when advocated and perceived threats are defined in identity and political rather than in distributional economic terms. Internationally, political nationalism places much less emphasis on reciprocity in economic terms and thus risks undermining rules-based international governance systems. This manifests itself in the growing economic tensions between the U.S. and China during the past years. 

This project examines how much state leaders and the mass public in the United States and China value reciprocity as a key cooperative principle in their bilateral economic relations. To answer this question, we rely on different perspectives in ‘International Political Economy’ (IPE) that yield diverging implications about the future of U.S.-Chinese economic relations. In one view, deviations from cooperative behaviour occur only if bilateral trade generates negative domestic economic repercussions. In the other, many citizens and state leaders support non-cooperative economic behaviour because the other country is seen as an international political competitor and political adversary. An analysis of these two perspectives will help us to better understand whether current nationalist grandstanding by some populist leaders is primarily a transient phenomenon or reflects a more deep-rooted public sentiment posing a more fundamental threat to the rules-based system. 

The proposed research examines the determinants of cooperative and non-cooperative behaviour in Chinese-U.S. economic relations on the macro and the micro level. The first part uses micro-level survey experiments to assess and explain the attitudes of American and Chinese citizens regarding their preferred foreign economic policy strategy towards the other country. The second part uses automated events data analysis to provide a historical macro-analysis of Chinese-U.S. economic interactions during the past decades. On both levels, the analysis will examine to what extent domestic economic repercussions of international economic exchange or ideological concerns about international political competition explain cooperative and non-cooperative behaviour. 

In summary, the main goals of the project encompass: 
•providing a systematic analysis of strategic economic policy interactions between China and the U.S.;
•identifying the varying cooperative and non-cooperative phases in Chinese-U.S. economic relations;
•gauging the influence of domestic economic and international political motives in these interactions;
•assessing the societal foundations of cooperative and non-cooperative policies in both countries.

River basins are seen as a natural laboratory for the integration of hydrological, ecological and geomorphological processes. Moving from morphological and functional analyses of dendritic geometries observed in Nature over a wide range of scales, the proposal addresses essential processes sustaining human life and societies taking place along fluvial dendritic structures suggesting that they can be predicted.  Population migrations and human settlements historically proceeded along river networks to follow water supply routes. Riparian systems, critically important ecosystems positioned along streams and rivers, play crucial roles in their watersheds The proposal hinges on the noteworthy scientific perspectives provided by ecohydrological studies of, including nutrient filtering, biogeochemical processing, shade and resource provisioning, and stream bank stabilization – and thus need be part of large-scale water resource management schemes. Devastating water-borne disease, such as cholera, and invading foreign species spread through water bodies linked by river networks. Although the dynamics of such systems has been extensively studied, existing approaches were mostly within the framework of mean-field or two-dimensional landscapes that ignore directionality of dispersal implied by the network acting as environmental matrix. How does connectivity within a river network affect the emergent spreading of water-borne infections?  Does the river basin act as a template for biodiversity? Are there hydrologic controls on the spreading of water-borne disease?  To answer such questions, the present proposal addresses, through direct and indirect data collections and comparative mathematical analyses, the study of: 
- biodiversity in the river basin (in particular, by studying comparatively observational data and dynamic models of freshwater fish in large and very large river systems, and of riparian vegetation); 
- hydrologic controls on cholera epidemics (and possibly of other water-borne disease); 
- zebra mussel (Dreissena polimorpha) invasions along river networks. 
This is intended to prove of crucial interest for understanding the functioning of river basins as a whole, including its ecosystem structure and function. 
Methods include mathematical models, ecological data collection and a basin-scale transport experiment. 
The most important scientific questions we plan to address deal with:
1)the recognition on solid theoretical and observational grounds of the nature and extent of hydrologic controls on the spreading of water-borne disease, typically cholera. If successfully proved, a predictive link of key features of epidemics – like for instance the total number of infected individuals, their spatial location and the timing of the infection peaks -- with hydrologic variables (like seasonally varying pools of resources for pathogens to disperse, or hydrologic travel- and life-times of pathogens between sites where susceptibles are above outbreak epidemics threshold) would prove of fundamental epidemiological significance, and of obviously large societal impact. It should be stressed that the populations exposed to water-borne disease typically live in the poor parts of the world, hence supporting the moral implications of the proposed research work;
2)the definition of capabilities of neutral ecological theory (where all species are equally capable of dispersing, colonizing and reproducing) to explain biodiversity in the presence of highly non-neutral constraints like the ones provided by the directional dispersal imposed by the fluvial corridors. This is particulary important to forefront ecological theory, because the neutral assumption is openly refused by researchers that attribute biodiversity largely to interspecific species’ interactions ignored therein. Preliminary results have shown that freshwater fish diversity patterns throughout the Mississippi-Missouri river system are well described by a neutral metacommunity model coupled with an appropriate habitat capacity distribution and dispersal kernel.  The current proposal plans to probe whether river network structure acts as an effective template for characterizing spatial attributes of biodiversity.  Objective estimates of average dispersal and of habitat capacities, under average runoff production or catastrophic events leading to recolonisation, will be investigated with a view to reliable predictions of large-scale spatial biodiversity patterns in riverine systems;
3)improved understanding of transport at basin scales. Should, for instance, the neutral theory prove successful beyond its initial domain both in two-dimensional and in dendritic ecosystems, and for a variety of different ecological processes, it would result that neutral metacommunity models would find crucial utility in a diverse suite of ecological processes.  Crucial to this end is a suitable assessment of the large-scale features of transport in the hydrological cycle, especially under catastrophic events. This framework would offer direct linkage from large-scale forcing, such as global climate changes, to biodiversity patterns. On this basis alone it is deemed that the proposed research might indeed prove groundbreaking, well beyond the field of hydrology.

The production of cement is responsible for approximately 5% of man-made CO2. The replacement of Portland cement by supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs) such as blast furnace slag, fly ash or calcined clay offers a very high potential to reduce these CO2 emissions. These SCMs react together with the Portland cement during hydration contributing mainly to the formation of additional calcium silicate hydrates (C-S-H). The high Al2O3 and SiO2 content of the SCMs lead to different C-S-H compositions with more silicon and aluminum than in Portland cements, which affects long-term stability and durability of such cements. Thus, obtaining in-depth understanding of the role of aluminum on C-S-H properties, and to develop adequate modelling approaches, which can quantify the high amount of aluminum in C-S-H, is crucial to predict the formed hydrate phase assemblages and their effects on durability. 
The present project aims at 
(i)identifying aluminum binding sites in C-S-H based on advanced solid state 27Al-NMR techniques; 
(ii)quantifying the relations between Ca/Si ratio and occupancies of the different Al sites; 
(iii)determining the relative stability and capacity of the different binding sites;
(iv)evaluating the stability of calcium, silicate and aluminate complexes;
(v)implementing Al-substitutions in C-S-H phases into thermodynamic models. 
The gained fundamental knowledge on the aluminum sites in the studied C-S-H phases and on aluminum substitution mechanisms may be transferred to calcium silicate hydrates in general and not only to those relevant to cement chemistry. We expect that consideration of C-A-S-H phases, i.e. aluminum-substituted C-S-H phases, in thermodynamic modelling will have a significant impact on the reliability of this approach for calculating the composition and phase assemblages of Portland cement – SCM blends and in the understanding of various factors affecting their durability. Reliable thermodynamic modeling tools provide a solid scientific background for predictions and testing of novel environmentally friendly and cost efficient cementitious materials. These modeling tools will enable us in the future to reliably predict the kind, amount and volume of hydration products also in silica-rich cementitious systems.
The knowledge on the relative stability of silicate and aluminate complexes contributes not only to a more accurate modeling of C-A-S-H solubility, but can be expected to shed light on the role of calcium silicate complexes as precursors for C-S-H nucleation and the role of aluminum in the retardation of alite dissolution. A question which is of great importance for setting and hardening of Portland cement based concrete. 
The cutting-edge modeling expertise with experimental knowledge and skills required for the project success are not available within a single research group. Therefore, we embark on an interdisciplinary collaboration between world-recognized experimental and modeling research groups working in the field of cement chemistry. Detailed solid-state 27Al MAS NMR studies are combined with the expertise on liquid phase analysis and the chemical thermodynamic modeling expertise at Empa and PSI.
The present project builds on the results and experiences of a previous very successful “sinergia” project which allowed to collect data on the solubility, structure, and composition of calcium-silicate hydrates incorporating aluminum “C(-A)-S-H” as a function of different parameters such as Ca/Si ratio, temperature, pH, aluminum, alkali and anion content (Swiss National Science Foundation Grant n° 130419).

This proposal is aimed at achieving a better understanding of what controls the evolution of galaxies over cosmic time. In particular, we propose investigations of four inter-related main topics:

• how galaxies are fed with inflowing gas to sustain their high star-formation rates at high redshift, including the hoped for first detection (in emission) of the gaseous cosmic web of gas at high redshifts;

• how star-forming galaxies launch galactic-scale winds and how these winds transport metals and likely also magnetic fields out into the intergalactic medium;

• how galaxies internally regulate their star-formation and, in particular, whether they do this through a simple gas reservoir, and what establishes the quasi-equilibrium that seems to characterize the evolution of the star-formation rate of galaxies over much of cosmic time;

• how the evolution of the active galactic nucleus (AGN) population is linked to that of the galaxy population and what can these population comparisons tell us about the links between galaxies and their central supermassive black holes of galaxies.

The projects are all observational, except part of the last, which is anyway very closely anchored on observational data.     The required observational data is mostly in hand or is associated with large amounts of guaranteed or otherwise previously awarded observing time.   The data flow for these projects is therefore essentially assured.  

In particular, this grant period sees the first exploitation of our guaranteed observing time (GTO) with the new MUSE instrument on the VLT, which was successfully commissioned at Paranal during 2014.  MUSE is an extremely powerful instrument that is ideally suited to answer many of our most pressing science questions.  We have also been awarded additional observing time on Hubble Space Telescope, ALMA and the VLT and are involved in a very large observing campaign on the IRAM Plateau de Bure mm-wave interferometer.

This proposal requests continued salary support for three PhD students and one postdoc position, plus travel support for these personnel.

The use of stabilised raw earth as a construction material is motivated by its low CO2 emissions compared to concrete, which instead accounts for about 5-9% of all anthropogenic CO2 emissions. Earthen materials cannot replace concrete for each purpose, but they may be used for non-load bearing concrete building block, as load-bearing walls for housing as well as for soft foundations if properly implemented. Raw earth is commonly stabilised with reactive calcium sources (lime or Portland cement) in order to increase its strength. However, this strong chemical hydraulic stabilisation, in addition to the use of material with high content of embodied CO2, hinders its re-use, and therefore increases largely its environmental footprint. While the stabilisation of raw earth materials (REMs ) with calcium hydraulic binders is relatively well understood, the current application aims to develop fundamental insights into durable stabilisation with magnesia for REMs. The project will enable the use of earth materials for future sustainable construction, using alternative stabiliser materials in order to reduce the overall environmental footprint of the construction industry. The following steps are planned to be investigated: 
i)Fundamental research on the effect of Mg-additives on workability, hardening, evolution of mineralogy and microstructure of stabilised raw earth materials;
ii)Evaluation of the effect of Mg-additives on the mechanical properties and on the reusability potential of such material;
iii)Optimisation of the resource chain via the implementation of a characterisation method for raw earths with regard to reactivity and content of magnesium.
The novelty of the project relies on the combination of a comprehensive study of chemical and physical processes involved in Mg stabilisation, together with the understanding and optimisation of criteria for the development of the new sustainable building materials. Assisted by a Ph.D. student, I will study in detail the hydration processes, the chemistry, the microstructure and the mechanical properties of REMs by means of a large palette of solid and liquid characterisation techniques available at the Concrete and Asphalt Laboratory (C&A Lab) at the Swiss Federal Laboratories for Materials Science and Technology (Empa) in Dübendorf. My expertise in advanced experimental characterisation and modelling, built up during my study of durability and stability of cement-clay interactions and of new Mg-Al-Si cements, will ensure the success of this project. The environmental impact of building materials will be evaluated by establishing full life cycle assessments at the co-host Institute of Construction and Infrastructure Management (IBI), particularly at the Chair of Sustainable Construction at the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology, Zürich (ETHZ). In order to limit the environmental footprint of MgO production, a new production route based on REMs containing clay material will be studied (planned cooperation with partners at the University of Glasgow and the Imperial College London). The thorough study from the nanoscale to the microstructure will provide a fundamental understanding of the role of Mg in REMs, a mandatory step to enable the development of low CO2 embodied alternative building materials.

Background: The vast majority of altitude research is conducted >3,000 m. While the robust hypoxic stimulus associated with such altitudes facilitates the uncovering of physiological responses, the findings may not apply to more moderate altitudes. The paucity of research conducted at moderate altitude (MA) represents a serious limitation given that, for instance, the number of people residing between 2,000 and 2,500 m is >5 times larger than that of people residing at higher altitudes. 

Aims: The overall aim of this project is to establish a comprehensive and integrative understanding of the effects of extended MA exposure. Specifically, we aim to assess the effects of MA on 
i)sympathetic activity and pulmonary ventilation; 
ii)arterial blood pressure regulation; 
iii)the pulmonary circulation; 
iv)metabolism and appetite; 
v)sleep architecture and nocturnal breathing;
vi)oxygen transport capacity and endurance performance. 

Methods: A total of 24 healthy, normally trained lowlanders (12 males, 12 females), aged between 20-35 years, and with a normal bodyweight will be recruited. During a one-week lead-in sojourn near sea-level, subjects’ habitual physical activity and diet will be assessed and baseline measurements will be conducted. Thereafter, participants will spend 4 weeks in the touristic mountain hut Masebene located in the South Tyrolian alps at 2267 m. The individual physical activity and diet from the lead-in sojourn will be reproduced throughout the MA sojourn. The experimental schedule of the MA sojourn will be as follows: Total hemoglobin mass will be measured every fourth day, whilst circulating erythropoietin and reticulocytes will be measured on the 2nd day and after the 2nd and 4th week, respectively. Endothelial function, arterial stiffness, pulmonary artery pressure, 24h blood pressure as well as appetite (along with circulating leptin and ghrelin), basal metabolic rate and oral glucose tolerance will be evaluated after each week, whereas metabolomics analysis of venous blood will be performed after the first and the last week. Muscle sympathetic nerve activity and pulmonary ventilation will be measured on the third day and at the end of the fourth week. Finally, polysomnography will be conducted during the first night, as well as during a night of the fourth week .
After the MA sojourn, subjects will return to sea-level, where the majority of measurements will be repeated over the course of the first week back at sea-level.
From the available literature, we estimated the effect size (Cohen’s d) for each of the aims to range from 0.58 to >1. A Cohen’s d of 0.5 is typically considered a medium effect, and the sample size needed to detect an effect size of 0.58 with 80% power and a significance level of 0.05 is 24. 

Scientific impact: Investigating whether the effects observed at high altitudes also occur at the much more populated/visited MA represents a crucial step to apply the wealth of insight gained from earlier studies to a broad population. Since MA may exert both beneficial and adverse health effects, as well as ergogenic effects, the outcome of our study will be of interest not only from a clinical, but also from a therapeutic and an athletic perspective.

The research objectives of the present project are the following:
A) To investigate the effect of randomness on low temperature magnetization of quantum spin systems;
B) To study U(1) symmetry breaking and violation of axial symmetry in magnetic insulators including quantum corrections.

The effective treatment of metastatic melanoma is an unmet clinical need since less than 50% of patients survive at 5 years after diagnosis of advanced disease. This dismal prognosis is mainly due to the formation of brain metastases for which no effective therapies are available. Of all cancer types, melanoma has the highest rate of cerebral tropism, with over 40% of advanced patients developing brain metastases.

Clinical observations have shown that patients above 70 years old have metastatic melanomas with the most aggressive prognostic features, which result in the highest mortality rate. Surprisingly, the impact of physiological aging on the pattern of melanoma metastases is largely unexplored when studying the biological mechanisms driving cancer cell dissemination. Pre-clinical and clinical studies have shown that blood vessels and lymphatics experience structural and functional changes during physiological aging, including compromised cell-cell junctions and increased vascular permeability, particularly in the blood brain barrier (BBB). This compromised vascular function coupled with the loss of vascular-identity genes increases the transmigration of circulating melanoma cells in the brain (i.e. extravasation). However, a detailed mechanistic explanation of the higher rate of melanoma cell transmigration through the vasculature of old patients is still lacking, partially due to the absence of reliable models that:
1) recapitulate features of vascular aging;
2) allow high-throughput (HT) parametric studies to identify drivers of melanoma cell extravasation.

To fill this gap, we propose to:
1) design a high-throughput system hosting 3D perfusable human BBB minitissues;
2) compare BBB models derived from human donors of different ages and validate them at the molecular and functional level by comparison with human brain tissue biopsies and published datasets;
3) analyze the biological mechanisms driving the age-dependent extravasation of melanoma cells and provide a list of candidate targets involved in melanoma extravasation for functional perturbation;
4) perform a CRISPR-based screening of the most promising targets and validate them

A key aspect of the proposal is the multidisciplinary integration of microvascular network (MVN) biofabrication, multi-omic characterization of human brain vasculature, and design of a data analysis methodology that exploits artificial intelligence to combine experimental data with existing databases to identify and rank pathways underlying melanoma extravasation. During the project we then expect to:
1) design and microfabricate a HT platform hosting human 3D minitissues that allows to perform large-scale experimental campaigns; this platform is fully integrated with HT biofabrication strategies and HT analytical techniques, and can find applications in any other field requiring screening of compounds or drugs (e.g. combination of chemotherapies on patient-derived cells);
2) provide a detailed molecular and functional analysis of the differences characterizing the human BBB during aging, which can have consequences in other disease models including neurodegeneration;
3) identify drivers of melanoma extravasation through the BBB by combining the molecular and functional profile of vasculature and cancer cells through HT live confocal imaging, automated quantification of local vascular permeability and cutting-edge spatial transcriptomics; the design of a novel data analysis pipeline is expected to discover potential mechanisms of extravasation also shared by immune cells during inflammatory processes or other phenomena involving immune cell mobilization;
4) validate the identified targets through a functional CRISPR screening and human tissue microarrays, which provide a higher clinical relevance and are often neglected in basic biology studies.

The DIAMO project is the extension of  the SNF project „Climatology of gravity wave-induced ozone perturbations“. The aim of the DIAMO project is the understanding of wave-mean flow interactions and their relationship to atmospheric composition changes on time scales from minutes to decades. The project is divided into three work packages WP1, WP2, and WP3. Each package is closely linked to the activities of the other packages.

WP1: Long-term monitoring of stratospheric ozone and dynamics
The IAP/Uni Bern has a long-term data set of stratospheric ozone above Bern from 1994 to 2010 with a time resolution of 3 min. Numerical simulations of WP2 permit us to link the  perturbations of atmospheric composition and atmospheric dynamics. We investigate:  
-Interannual and seasonal modulation of atmospheric waves above Bern;
-Climatology  of fluctuations and intraseasonal oscillations of stratospheric ozone;
-Trend study of stratospheric ozone, temperature, and dynamics above Bern.

WP2: Simulation of gravity waves and wave-induced changes of atmospheric composition and mean flow
Numerical simulations are  performed with the Weather Research and Forecasting Model (WRF): 
-Interaction of atmospheric gravity waves and the mean flow;
-Interplay between gravity waves, cloud formation and precipitation;
-Impact of gravity waves on stratospheric ozone;
-Intercomparison of simulations and observations (from IAP database).

WP3: Spaced antenna measurements for 3D monitoring of atmospheric motions 
The DIAMO project utilizes spaced antenna and multibeam data of ground-based microwave radiometers for time lag measurement of coherent signatures of ozone perturbations:      
-3D monitoring of the propagation of atmospheric gravity waves through the stratosphere and lower mesosphere; 
-Determination of  middle atmospheric winds by means of the drift velocity of ozone irregularities.

Between the 16th and 17th centuries, the stuccatori from Canton Ticino were requested in all of Europe to take part in the decoration of the main building projects. Their methods of organizing the work, their ability to collaborate with other artists and workers, their attention to updating formal requirements and the strong tie to their native soil (maintaining a continuous renewal of ideas and workforce) represented their strengths. These qualities probably would not have been sufficient if they were not accompanied also by a particular ability in mastering the challenging art of stucco.

Despite much research and numerous publications that have explored the works of these artists and their cultural context, there has never been attention on the art and industry of these stuccatori, and how their practical and technical “know-how” contributed to the great professional and artistic success that they enjoyed. It is still not known if there is a typical art of stucco specific to Canton Ticino and to the Region of the lakes or if each artist or “family” interpreted the use of materials and techniques in their own way.

This research project intends to analyse the technique and the artistic process with which three "botteghe" (the Silva, the Tencalla and the Casella of Carona), all active between the 16th and 17th centuries, created important works of art in their homelands, in order to understand if it is possible to individuate a common modus operandi or if each “family” worked according to their own established methods. Studying the artistic process and not only the finished “products” will lead to a better understanding of stucco decoration, placing this art form in a wider cultural framework.  

The study of the techniques with which some important stucco decoration was created will help to:
-  understand the artistic process, going beyond a purely formal reading of the stucco decoration;
- expand with new data the knowledge of the art historians, verifying the existence of “schools” and working groups, contributing to the clarification of attribution and dating;
- compare the decoration created by these artists outside of Ticino, and in this way evaluate the role of the technique (in its variations and endurances) in the realization of their works of art.

The research will be developed bringing together three different disciplines (art history and material culture, visual investigation of artistic techniques and practices, and archaeometry and materials science) to put in perspective information gathered from a variety of sources:
- written documents pertaining to the materials and the techniques used, preserved mainly in parish archives (including building books, contracts, bills, invoices, and receipts), until now little explored, but potentially a very rich source of  information about the history of the design and the making of stucco;
- direct examination of the works of art and their technical and executive features;
- material analyses (chemical, physical and petrographical) of the works of art to understand the characteristics and properties of the materials used, and to compare, with an archaeological and interdisciplinary approach, the analytical data with the historical and materials information.

Multiphoton microscopy is today unrivalled among other optical approaches in intravital imaging, as it allows imaging of labelled structures and proteins while providing large optical penetration depth with sub-cellular axial resolution, keeping comparatively low photobleaching levels. These advantages, along with the possibility of exciting genetically encoded calcium and voltage indicators, have made multiphoton microscopy the method of choice for neuronal studies in rodents to decipher brain function and understand neuronal diseases. Optical sampling at high speeds is of paramount importance for accurately capturing neural activity, as the characteristic timescales for action potentials are of the order of a few milliseconds, while Ca transients correlate in the 100 ms range. Current imaging technologies do not sufficiently combine fast recording rates with volumetric sampling, which is equally important to fully understand the inherently three-dimensional neuronal circuitry. With this WEAVE project, we aim to advance and tailor the spectro-temporal laser imaging by diffractive excitation technique previously introduced by one partner to the challenges of neurophotonics by:
i)acquiring volumetric imaging of layered neuronal structures (cortex, intestine) at the inherent timescales of their functional processes (20Hz volume rate);

ii)developing a dedicated near infrared source to image and benefit from the matured GCaMP family of fluorescence proteins; 
iii)analyzing and optimizing signal yield and sample damage introducing rapid adaptive scanning and sampling protocols;
iv)correcting wavefront distortions and accounting for specimen movements by employing nonlinear fiduciary markers embedded in tissue depth.

To achieve these goals, the ADAPT research partners have joined in a synergetic WEAVE consortium with laser physicist and inventor of spectro-temporal Multiphoton imaging Sebastian Karpf (GER) to build a volumetric imaging system at 940 nm that is perfectly tuned to image GFP-like molecules at 20 Hz volumetric imaging; nanophotonics and nonlinear optics expert Luigi Bonacina (CH) to advance, assess, and translate the disruptive technology to adaptive and targeted sampling exploiting the active modulation of the source and bespoke solutions for three-dimensional scanning. Further, LB will set-up a dedicated SHG detection channel to collect information from a nonlinear guide star to counteract propagation-induced wavefront distortions and account for sample movements (heartbeat, breathing). Finally, neurophysiology expert Pieter Vanden Berghe (BE) will study together with LB the advantages brought forth by the high-speed, advanced scanning protocols to optimize and investigate signal yield and sample damage as compared to state-of-the-art femtosecond systems. PVB will also apply the ADAPT technology to volumetric neuronal activity imaging using GCaMP to push the current boundaries of neuronal intravital imaging.

Low-energy observables measured to high precision provide stringent tests of the Standard Model of particle physics (SM). Even though we expect the SM to be an effective-field theory (EFT) valid in a limited
domain of energy scales, no convincing signs of its breakdown have been seen so far. There are, however, a few observations involving muons which hint at  physics beyond the Standard Model (BSM). Here I consider the following are "muon anomalies": 

- muon g-2: 3 to 4 standard-deviations discrepancy between the SM prediction and the experimental value of the muon anomalous magnetic moment;

- proton radius puzzle: 5.6 standard-deviations discrepancy in the extractions of the proton charge radius  from electron-proton scattering and hydrogen spectroscopy, as combined in the CODATA '14 recommended value, versus the new precise value from the muonic-hydrogen Lamb shift.

In both of these, the uncertainty in the knowledge of hadronic corrections is the leading one on the SM side. The aim of this research proposal is to systematically improve the evaluation of these hadronic corrections to muon anomalies and thereby reduce the uncertainty of the SM predictions. 

The running coupling of the strong interaction --- quantum chromodynamics (QCD) --- prohibits a perturbative treatment at low energies. Therefore, one has to resort to lattice QCD, dispersion relations (DRs) or low-energy EFTs to access the hadronic corrections. In this project, the main tools will be DRs and baryon chiral perturbation theory (BChPT), a low-energy EFT of QCD in terms of hadronic degrees of freedom.

The research proposal can be divided into three topical areas: 

A) Hadronic contributions to the muon g-2 via Schwinger's sum rule;

B) Nucleon structure from theory and experiment in view of the muonic-hydrogen hyperfine-splitting experiments;

C) Beyond Standard Model physics (lepton flavour violation and axions);

with A and B the focus projects. The fields of muonic atoms and the muon anomalous magnetic moment (AMM) are very timely, because new experimental results with improved precision are expected in the next few years, for instance, from the Fermilab E989 experiment for muon g-2 and the CREMA Collaboration, which aims to measure the ground-state hyperfine splitting (HFS) in muonic hydrogen at the Paul Scherrer Institute (PSI). The CREMA Collaboration, however, crucially relies on quantitative theory guidance to shrink the searching range for the narrow hyperfine transition and make their ambitious measurement possible.
Therefore, some of the key objectives for this Ambizione grant are: 

1. Re-evaluation of the pseudoscalar contributions to the muon g-2 with Schwinger's sum rule;

2. Feasibility study for the measurement of muon structure functions (SFs) in inelastic electron-muon scattering as empirical input for the Schwinger sum rule;

3. A global fit of inelastic nucleon SFs, including new data from e.g. the Jefferson Lab "Spin Physics Program", with strong emphasis on the implementation of rigorous theoretical constraints;

4. Proton-structure corrections to the muonic-hydrogen spectrum in view of forthcoming HFS experiments:

a) dispersive evaluation based on the new global fit of proton spin SFs; 

b) disentangling the Zemach-radius and proton-polarizability contributions.

The work on the Schwinger sum rule will provide a new model-independent approach for the evaluation of the notoriously-difficult hadronic contributions to muon g-2 and, most importantly, refine the hadronic light-by-light contributions. The new global fit of nucleon SFs will implement the theoretical constraints missed in the existing parametrizations (e.g., in the Bosted-Christy parametrizations) and account for new experimental data. This will allow for a more accurate evaluation of the proton-structure effects in muonic hydrogen, and thereby contribute to the successful performance of the muonic-hydrogen HFS experiments. It will, in particular, shed new light on the discrepancy between the BChPT prediction and dispersive evaluations of the proton-polarizability contribution to the muonic-hydrogen HFS. The forthcoming PSI measurement of the muonic-hydrogen ground-state HFS will, in turn, provide a determination of the spin-dependent two-photon-exchange (TPE) effect with unprecedented precision, and thus, provide us with novel insights and constraints on the proton spin structure.

Current knowledge: Phytogenic compounds are of interest as feed additives for ruminants because they potentially can serve as: i) natural methane inhibitors, thereby decreasing the global greenhouse gas burden (i.e., promoting ecological health), and ii) health-promoters and natural substitutes for in-feed antibiotics, (i.e., promoting animal health).
The currently available information about the impact of phytogenic compounds as feed additives can be summarised as follows: i) Phytogenic compounds fed over a short period are able to decrease methane formation deriving from ruminants; ii) The methane-mitigating properties of phytogenic compounds seem to be type-, dosage- and diet-dependent, with inter-species differences between ruminants being most likely ; iii) Phytogenic compounds in the form of phenolic compounds seem to have the most favourable effects; iv) phenolic compounds reduce ruminal bloat and the gastrointestinal burden due to nematodes; while other health-improving effects are indicated; and v) some (dosages of) phenolic compounds impair animal health even over short time periods.
Gaps in knowledge: Following issues require further study: i) Identification of phenolic compounds/compound mixtures effective in mitigating CH4 emission while improving ruminants’ health and productivity; ii) Persistence of the methane-mitigating action of phenolic compounds when fed over long time periods; iii) Long-term effects of phenolic compounds on animal health and productivity; iv) Effects of phenolic compounds on reproduction, and development of foetal, newborn and growing animals; and v) Effects of phenolic compounds on the quality of animal source food.
Research Plan: The project focuses on the following four objectives (a-d) achieved by a total of five activities:
a)Identification of promising phenolic compounds and compound mixtures, as well as their ideal dosage with different diets commonly fed to ruminants regarding CH4 mitigation and nutrient fermentation by ruminal microbes and generating possible explanations of the mode of action, i.e., direct vs. indirect effect, of the phenolic compounds in exerting CH4-mitigation in the rumen; 
b)Determination of the long-term impact of dietary administration of phenolic compounds/compound mixtures on enteric CH4 formation, animal health and productivity; 
c)Determination of the impact of dietary administration of phenolic compounds /compound mixtures to ruminants before and during pregnancy and lactation as well as evaluating the effects of phenolic compounds when supplemented to the diet of lactating animals on growth and performance of the offspring;
d)Determination of the effects of phenolic compounds on the quality of animal source food.
Importance and Impact: So far, many ruminant nutrition studies have been carried out to investigate the CH4-mitigating effects of phenolic compounds or their impact on health aspects such as ruminal bloat and gastrointestinal nematodes. However, studies combining both aspects, i.e., the impact on animal and ecological health of prolonged administration of phenolic compounds to ruminants, have not been sufficiently investigated. Although phenolic compounds as feed additive seem promising from the results currently generated, greater knowledge of aspects such as their long-term impact on animal health, productivity and the quality of animal source food is imperative before such dietary additives can be applied in livestock husbandry.
